<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ascalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12 August 1099</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> was fought on 12 August 1099 near Ascalon on the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, soon after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> and the other crusader leaders surprised and routed a Fatimid army led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>. The victory secured the new crusader conquests, although Ascalon itself remained Fatimid until 1153.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon.jpg" alt="Battle of Ascalon" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Victory Schnetz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1099_Ascalon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
